The 8191-pixel width cap in CAMSS belongs to the test pattern generator, not to the capture path

The rear camera has a 9248x6944 mode -the native 64 Mpx of the IMX682- and the
pipeline refused to start:

    v4l2_subdev_link_validate_default: width does not match
                                       (source 9248, sink 8191)
    link was "imx682 12-001a":0 -> "msm_csiphy0":0
    Failed to start media pipeline: -32

8191 is 2^13-1, and that thirteen-bit limit DOES exist... but somewhere else. It
belongs to the CSID TEST PATTERN GENERATOR, which programs the width into a
thirteen-bit field and moreover already masks it itself:

    camss-csid-gen2.c:
        val |= (input_format->width & 0x1fff) << TPG_DT_n_CFG_0_FRAME_WIDTH;

On the REAL path through RDI the width is not programmed into any register: the
VFE write master receives

    camss-vfe-480.c:
        #define WM_IMAGE_CFG_0_DEFAULT_WIDTH  (0xFFFF)
        writel_relaxed(WM_IMAGE_CFG_0_DEFAULT_WIDTH,
                       vfe->base + VFE_BUS_WM_IMAGE_CFG_0(wm));

that is, the maximum of a sixteen-bit field, without looking at the format. RDI
is a byte dump and does not need to know the width.

Even so, 8191 is copied verbatim as a global cap in camss-video.c,
camss-csiphy.c, camss-csid.c and camss-vfe.c, where it blocks valid sensors. The
IMX682 is not unusual: any sensor wider than 8191 pixels -and there are plenty of
48 and 64 Mpx ones- runs into this.

It is raised to 16383. That amply covers the 9248 of the IMX682 and leaves the
width times height product far from overflowing 32 bits in the image size
calculations. CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_HEIGHT_PIX is left alone; it belongs to the path
with processing and has its own limit.

HOW IT IS CHECKED
-----------------
The other four rear camera modes already capture and must not change. The
9248x6944 one goes from "Failed to start media pipeline: -32" to delivering
packets. If the limit really were a hardware one, the large mode would start but
would give garbage or zero packets, which is perfectly distinguishable.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-video.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-video.c
@@ -20,9 +20,30 @@
 #include "camss.h"
 
 #define CAMSS_FRAME_MIN_WIDTH		1
-#define CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_WIDTH		8191
+/*
+ * 8191 is 2^13-1, and that thirteen-bit limit is NOT from the capture path: it is
+ * from the CSID PATTERN GENERATOR, which programs the width in a thirteen-bit field
+ * bits y ya se enmascara solo:
+ *
+ *   camss-csid-gen2.c:  val |= (input_format->width & 0x1fff) << ...FRAME_WIDTH;
+ *
+ * On the real path through RDI the width is not programmed anywhere: the VFE write
+ * master receives WM_IMAGE_CFG_0_DEFAULT_WIDTH = 0xFFFF without looking at the
+ * format. Even so the 8191 was copied as a global cap in camss-video, csiphy,
+ * csid y vfe, y ahi bloquea sensores perfectamente validos.
+ *
+ * The symptom on the surya, with the 64 Mpx mode of the rear camera:
+ *
+ *   v4l2_subdev_link_validate_default: width does not match
+ *                                      (source 9248, sink 8191)
+ *   Failed to start media pipeline: -32
+ *
+ * It is raised to 16383, which amply covers the 9248 of the IMX682 and keeps the product
+ * width x height far from overflowing 32 bits in the size calculations.
+ */
+#define CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_WIDTH		16383
 #define CAMSS_FRAME_MIN_HEIGHT		1
-#define CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_HEIGHT_RDI	8191
+#define CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_HEIGHT_RDI	16383
 #define CAMSS_FRAME_MAX_HEIGHT_PIX	4096
 
 /* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csiphy.c
@@ -382,8 +382,8 @@
 		if (i >= csiphy->res->formats->nformats)
 			fmt->code = MEDIA_BUS_FMT_UYVY8_1X16;
 
-		fmt->width = clamp_t(u32, fmt->width, 1, 8191);
-		fmt->height = clamp_t(u32, fmt->height, 1, 8191);
+		fmt->width = clamp_t(u32, fmt->width, 1, 16383);
+		fmt->height = clamp_t(u32, fmt->height, 1, 16383);
 
 		fmt->field = V4L2_FIELD_NONE;
 		fmt->colorspace = V4L2_COLORSPACE_SRGB;
